//? Title: Jumping along corridors without skipping a turn
//? Subtitle: The historical jump_point_search API as checked four-neighbor corridor A*
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A jump can save queue work along a corridor, but jumping past a junction can
lose the only route. This report preserves the original orthogonal-grid API and
two-by-two example, explains a completeness correction, and derives the safe
corridor contraction used by its native implementation. Returned paths include
every unit step. This corridor-jump variant does not implement the symmetry
pruning of published Jump Point Search, and no open-room acceleration claim is
made.
)

\toc

> A three-cell decision matters

Place a vertical exit above the middle of a horizontal passage. Starting at its
left end, a ray that jumps directly to the right wall skips the only upward
turn. The corrected native search retains any cell where a perpendicular move is
possible. The diagram depicts the actual returned route coordinates; it is a
route summary, not a recorded queue history.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/jump_point_junction.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/jump_point_search.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
#include <stdexcept>
#include <cmath>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    const std::vector<std::vector<bool>> g = {{true, false, true},
                                              {false, false, false},
                                              {true, true, true}};
    const auto r = dm::algorithms::jump_point_search(g, {1, 0}, {0, 1});
    if (!r.found || r.cost != 2)
        throw std::runtime_error("junction route");
    std::print("| route index | row | column |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < r.path.size(); ++i)
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} |\n", i, r.path[i].first, r.path[i].second);
    dm::graphics::Diagram d;
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < r.path.size(); ++i) {
        const auto[row, col] = r.path[i];
        d.node(std::to_string(i), {5. * i, 0},
               std::format("Route {}\n({},{})", i, row, col));
        if (i)
            d.edge(std::to_string(i - 1), std::to_string(i));
    }
    d.save_svg("build/jump_point_junction.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| route index | row | column |
| ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 0 | 1 | 0 |
| 1 | 1 | 1 |
| 2 | 0 | 1 |
\image(figures/jump_point_junction.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The returned route retains the intermediate junction.)
\alttext(Three route nodes show the left passage cell, its central junction and the upward goal, with two unit moves.)

> Contract only a straight corridor

A nonterminal degree-two cell whose two free neighbors are opposite cannot be a
turn on a shortest path. A shortest simple route must pass straight through it.
Replacing a sequence of such cells by one edge weighted by its number of unit
moves preserves shortest-path cost. Retain junctions, corners, dead ends, the
start and the goal. The implementation scans down, up, right and left from each
expanded state, stopping at a possible perpendicular turn, the goal, or the last
free cell before an obstruction.

For a macro edge from u to v with length L, Manhattan distance satisfies

$$
h(u)\leq L+h(v).
$$
\alttext(The Manhattan lower bound at an edge origin is at most the edge length plus the lower bound at its endpoint.)

Thus Manhattan remains consistent on the retained graph. A* orders by g+h,
lazily rejects stale entries, and preserves the first parent on equal costs;
heap ties use row-major cell identity. Restoring all unit cells from the
selected axis-aligned macro edges gives the native inclusive route. The old
wall-to-wall shortcut lacked this junction invariant and could return failure on
the first example.

> Keep the original open two-by-two example

The original report used an obstacle-free two-by-two grid, start (0,0), goal
(1,1), and asserted only success through its exit status. Here the program
exposes the actual cost and full returned route. Coordinates are (row,column),
true means blocked, and the native renderer places rows downward.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/jump_point_original.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/jump_point_search.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
#include <stdexcept>
#include <cmath>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    const std::vector<std::vector<bool>> g = {{false, false}, {false, false}};
    const auto r = dm::algorithms::jump_point_search(g, {0, 0}, {1, 1});
    std::print("| found | cost | inclusive route cells |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| {} | {} | {} |\n",
               r.found, r.cost, r.path.size());
    for (const auto[row, col] : r.path)
        std::print("({}, {})\n", row, col);
    if (!r.found || r.cost != 2 || r.path.size() != 3)
        throw std::runtime_error("original case");
    dm::algorithms::plot_grid_route(g, r, {0, 0}, {1, 1}).title(
        "Original two-by-two grid: a shortest route").save_svg(
        "build/jump_point_original.svg");
}
```
