> Boundaries and the repaired junction

Blocked or out-of-bounds terminals return an empty path, zero cost and
found=false; cost zero alone is not a reachability test. A free start equal to
goal succeeds with one route cell and cost zero. Empty and ragged grids throw.
Rows, columns and total cells must fit the checked integer cell-id domain before
label allocation. Grid input is borrowed during the call and unchanged; returned
routes own their coordinates.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/jump_point_controls.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/jump_point_search.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
#include <stdexcept>
#include <cmath>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    const std::vector<std::vector<bool>> junction = {{true, false, true},
                                                     {false, false, false},
                                                     {true, true, true}};
    const auto show =
        [](const char *name, const dm::algorithms::GridPathResult & r) {
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", name, r.found, r.cost,
                   r.path.size());
    };
    std::print("| control | found | cost | route cells |\n| --- | --- | ---: | ---: |\n");
    show("required junction",
         dm::algorithms::jump_point_search(junction, {1, 0}, {0, 1}));
    show("blocked start",
         dm::algorithms::jump_point_search(junction, {0, 0}, {0, 1}));
    show("outside goal",
         dm::algorithms::jump_point_search(junction, {1, 0}, {-1, 1}));
    show("same free cell",
         dm::algorithms::jump_point_search(junction, {1, 1}, {1, 1}));
    show("disconnected",
         dm::algorithms::jump_point_search({{false, true, false}}, {0, 0},
                                           {0, 2}));
    bool empty = false, ragged = false;
    try {
        (void) dm::algorithms::jump_point_search({}, {0, 0}, {0, 0});
    } catch (const std::invalid_argument &) {
        empty = true;
    }
    try {
        (void) dm::algorithms::jump_point_search({{false}, {false, false}},
                                                 {0, 0}, {1, 0});
    } catch (const std::invalid_argument &) {
        ragged = true;
    }
    if (!empty || !ragged)
        throw std::runtime_error("shape validation");
    std::print("\nEmpty and ragged grids reject before search.\n");
    const auto r = dm::algorithms::jump_point_search(junction, {1, 0}, {0, 1});
    dm::algorithms::plot_grid_route(junction, r, {1, 0}, {0, 1}).title(
        "Keep the turn instead of jumping to the wall").save_svg(
        "build/jump_point_controls.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| control | found | cost | route cells |
| --- | --- | ---: | ---: |
| required junction | true | 2 | 3 |
| blocked start | false | 0 | 0 |
| outside goal | false | 0 | 0 |
| same free cell | true | 0 | 1 |
| disconnected | false | 0 | 0 |

Empty and ragged grids reject before search.
\image(figures/jump_point_controls.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The repaired route takes a turn that wall-only jumping skipped.)
\alttext(Blocked cells surround a T junction; the blue route turns upward through the middle cell to reach the goal.)

> Returned output still grows with corridor length

Even if a whole straight corridor becomes one macro edge, this API returns every
original cell. The following controls measure returned route cost and cell
count, not queue expansions or elapsed speed. Output alone requires linear work
and storage in path length. In an open room every neighboring cell permits a
perpendicular turn, so this conservative variant retains all those opportunities
rather than applying JPS symmetry pruning.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/jump_point_output.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/jump_point_search.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
#include <stdexcept>
#include <cmath>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    std::vector<double> x, y;
    std::print("| corridor cells | actual cost | returned cells |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (int n = 8; n <= 1024; n *= 2) {
        const std::vector<std::vector<bool>> g(1, std::vector<bool>(n, false));
        const auto r = dm::algorithms::jump_point_search(g, {0, 0}, {0, n - 1});
        if (!r.found || r.cost != n - 1 || r.path.size() != std::size_t(n))
            throw std::runtime_error("corridor output");
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} |\n", n, r.cost, r.path.size());
        x.push_back(std::log2(n));
        y.push_back(std::log2(r.path.size()));
    }
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    p.line(x, y, "route cells", {37, 99, 235}, 2).points(x, y, "",
                                                         {37, 99, 235}, 4).title("Complete output grows with corridor length").x_label("log2(corridor cells)").y_label("log2(returned route cells)").y_limits(2, 12).save_svg("build/jump_point_output.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| corridor cells | actual cost | returned cells |
| ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 8 | 7 | 8 |
| 16 | 15 | 16 |
| 32 | 31 | 32 |
| 64 | 63 | 64 |
| 128 | 127 | 128 |
| 256 | 255 | 256 |
| 512 | 511 | 512 |
| 1024 | 1023 | 1024 |
\image(figures/jump_point_output.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Complete returned routes grow linearly even when a corridor can be contracted.)
\alttext(On logarithmic axes, returned cell count equals corridor cell count for every measured case; these values are output sizes rather than search work.)

For N grid cells and maximum scanned ray length L, the implementation has a
bound O(NL+N log(N+1)) and O(N) label/heap/output storage. This is a
conservative corridor method; claiming the published JPS pruning guarantees for
it would be incorrect. It introduces no external dataset because exhaustive
finite grids provide stronger controlled correctness checks for this example.

All 512 three-by-three occupancies and every start/goal pair passed the native
BFS comparison. A separate Python BFS verified 1,000 random grid results,
including unit-step route feasibility, blocked endpoints and unreachable
controls. The shared ten-report build passed 131 native tests, 38 directly
instrumented address/undefined-behaviour sanitizer tests, 89 compiled public
examples, documentation coverage for all 388 scoped definitions and ten
representative clangd hovers. All four complete C++ report programs executed
successfully and their native figures were visually reviewed. Batch receipts are
retained under `build/mepml-conversion/batch-174-183/`.

> References

Hart, Nilsson and Raphael (1968), “A Formal Basis for the Heuristic
Determination of Minimum Cost Paths”,
[doi:10.1109/TSSC.1968.300136|https://doi.org/10.1109/TSSC.1968.300136],
supplies the A* background. Harabor and Grastien (2011),
[Online Graph Pruning for Pathfinding on Grid Maps|https://ojs.aaai.org/index.php/AAAI/article/view/7994],
describes published Jump Point Search. This report explicitly studies the
historical API's four-neighbor corridor variant rather than attributing full JPS
symmetry pruning to it.
